  - #303 merged as 2c768cc, after going green with no open review threads.
  - #304 opened for Phase 2 PRs 2.3 and 2.4: clock retargeting, show_soc and the SoC checks. It was rebased onto main, and 432 local tests pass on it. It
    merges automatically once CI is green and no review threads are open.
  - Phase 3 PRs 3.1 and 3.2 are written and tested (64 new tests) on feat/soc-efficiency. The PR opens once the local regression run finishes and the branch
    is rebased onto #304.

  What Phase 3 has shown so far:

  - The annex model reproduces exactly. With the annex's own throughput figures, the analyzer matches the Data Annex on all 18 profiles. Far flight comes out
    at 16.18 against the argument document's 16.2, and air superiority at 16.62 against 17.3, which is inside the annex's 4% cross-check.
  - Orin runs out of memory bandwidth in far flight. The mission needs 306 GB/s of DRAM traffic, which is more than Orin's 204.8 GB/s peak. That is 2.3× what
    Orin can sustain, so the regime fails on memory before compute is considered.
  - The Orin calibrations source only two per-engine efficiencies:
    - GPU FP32 GEMM runs at 28–40% of peak.
    - DLA INT8 convolution runs at about 2% of peak. These were batch-1 single-layer runs, so launch overhead dominates.

    Neither is a format the workload's stages actually run in. As a result, every stage on the per-engine path is a stated gap, and I have not filled any in
    with guesses. This is the same approach as the Phase 2 area check.
  - Two sources were added to the IP library. The Cortex-A78AE per-core SIMD rates come from Arm's Software Optimization Guide (PJDOC-466751330-14665
    (https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5fb7d95ed77dd807b9a80c79)), where FMLA and SDOT each issue 2 per cycle. The LPDDR5 bandwidth of 204.8 GB/s
    comes from the Orin Technical Brief.

  Decision for you before PR 3.3 (the power roll-up): at Samsung 8LPP, the process-node catalog has no DRAM I/O energy, no SRAM access energy and no NoC
  energy. The repo also has no simple per-architecture overhead factor to apply. So Orin's power will mostly be gaps too, and the "Orin at 25 W versus the
  measured 101.8 GOPS/W" check can't be evaluated honestly yet.

  The alternative is a per-engine table labelled THEORETICAL and derived from the annex's class rates. It would rest on one stated assumption:

  - Class A runs on the GPU in INT8.
  - Class B runs on the GPU in FP32.
  - Class C runs on a CPU core.

  That would give the per-engine and power paths real numbers, clearly labelled as derived. Unless you say otherwise, I'll keep the strict path.

  Sources:
  - Arm Cortex-A78AE Software Optimization Guide (https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5fb7d95ed77dd807b9a80c79)
  - Arm Cortex-A78AE Software Optimization Guide (developer.arm.com) (https://developer.arm.com/documentation/PJDOC-466751330-14665/0600)

